Define the snapshots points as in~\cref{eq:equispaced}, and let $p_*$ be the new parameter value for which we want to solve the eigenvalue problem~\cref{eq:eig}. Under \Cref{assumption}, it holds that, if $p_* \in [-1,1]$ then
\begin{equation}
\bigl\| (I-XX^{\dagger})\boldsymbol{x}(p_*) \bigr\|_2
\le
2\
\frac{\kappa_{\rho}}{1 - \rho^{-1}}\left(
1 +
C(r,R)\sqrt{(R+1)}
\right)\rho^{-(R+1)}
\end{equation}
else if $p_* \in (1, \frac{\rho + \rho^{-1}}{2})$ then 
\begin{equation}
\bigl\| (I-XX^{\dagger})\boldsymbol{x}(p_*) \bigr\|_2
\le
2 \kappa_{\rho}
\left(
\frac{1}{1 - \delta}
+
\frac{C(r,R)}{(1 - \rho^{-1})\sqrt{\rho^2\delta^2-1}}
\right)
\, \delta^{R+1},
\end{equation}
where $
C(r,R) = \frac{5\sqrt{5}\sqrt{2R+1}\sqrt{r}}{\sqrt{2(r-1)}}
$, for any $R \le \frac{1}{2}\sqrt{r-1},\ \delta = \frac{p_* + \sqrt{p_*^2 - 1}}{\rho} < 1.$

We follow the argument in \cite[Thm $5$]{Gui2024}. Let $\boldsymbol{p}(t)$ be a vector-valued polynomial. Assume also that $\boldsymbol{p}(t) \in \mbox{span}(X)$ for all $t$. Since the orthogonal projection $XX^{\dagger}\boldsymbol{x}(p_*)$ finds the closest point in $\mbox{span}(X)$ to $\boldsymbol{x}(p_*)$, we have
    \[ \bigl\| (I-XX^{\dagger})\boldsymbol{x}(p_*) \bigr\|_2
\le \|\boldsymbol{x}(p_*)-\boldsymbol{p}(p_*)\|_2.\]
In particular, an upper bound on $\bigl\| (I-XX^{\dagger})\boldsymbol{x}(p_*) \bigr\|_2$ is given by the best polynomial approximation in $p_*$. The result in \cite[Thm $5$]{Gui2024} gives un upper bound on this quantity.

Let $\boldsymbol{x}_R(t) = \sum_{k=0}^R \boldsymbol{c}_k T_k(t)$ be the truncated Chebyshev series 
(which converges geometrically by the analyticity assumption~\cite[Thm. 8.2]{Tre2020})
of $\boldsymbol{x}(t)$ up to degree $R$, and let $\boldsymbol{p}_R(t) = \sum_{k=0}^R \boldsymbol{a}_k T_k(t) \in \text{span}(X)$ be the least-square polynomial constructed on the snapshot points.
According to \cite[Lemma 4]{Gui2024} the coefficients satisfy $\|\boldsymbol{c}_k\|_2 \le 2\kappa_\rho \rho^{-k}$. Furthermore, defining $C_x = [\boldsymbol{c}_0, \dots, \boldsymbol{c}_R]$, $C_p = [\boldsymbol{a}_0, \dots, \boldsymbol{a}_R]$, and $\boldsymbol{q}_R(t) = [q_0(t), \dots, q_R(t)]^T$, we have that 
\begin{equation}\@bsphack
