    Consider a unit positive Borel measure $\mu$ on $[-1,1]$. Define the snapshot points $p_0, \dots, p_{r-1}$ such that $p_i \neq p_j$ and the empirical measure
    \[ \mu_r = \frac1r\sum_{i = 0}^{r-1} \delta(p_i)\]
    converges to $\mu$ in the weak-star topology as $r\rightarrow \infty$. Let $p_*$ be a new parameter value (inside or outside $[-1,1]$) such that $U^\mu(p_*)<+\infty$.
    Then,
    \[ \limsup_{r \rightarrow \infty}\bigl\| (I-XX^{\dagger})\boldsymbol{x}(p_*) \bigr\|_2^{1/r} \leq e^{-c} <1 \]
    for some constant $c>0$ that may depend on $p_*$,  under the assumption that $\rho$ in \Cref{assumption} is large enough such that 
    \[ \max_{z \in \partial E_{\rho}}U^{\mu}(z)  <U^{\mu}(p_*).\]

The proof combines the Hermite interpolation formula \cite[Eq. 4.2]{Saf2010} \cite[\S 11]{Tre2020}. with logarithmic potential theory \cite{Saf2010}
\cite[\S 12]{Tre2020}. Define
\[ \Pi_r(z) = \prod_{i=0}^{r-1} (z - p_i).\]
According to \cite[Thm. 11.1]{Tre2020}, the polynomial interpolant at the snapshot points can be written as 
\[ \boldsymbol{p}(p_*) = \frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{\Gamma}\frac{\boldsymbol{x}(z)(\Pi_r(z)-\Pi_r(p_*))}{\Pi_r(z)(z-p_*)}dz\]
where $\Gamma$ is the boundary of the Bernstein ellipse in \Cref{assumption}.
Note that $\boldsymbol{p}(p_*) \in \mbox{span}(X)$. The error $ \boldsymbol{x}(p_*) -\boldsymbol{p}(p_*)$ satisfies
\[ \boldsymbol{x}(p_*) -\boldsymbol{p}(p_*) = \frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{\Gamma}\frac{\boldsymbol{x}(z)\Pi_r(p_*)}{\Pi_r(z)(z-p_*)}dz.\]
 It follows that
\begin{align*} \bigl\| (I-XX^{\dagger})\boldsymbol{x}(p_*) \bigr\|_2 &\le   \bigl\| \boldsymbol{x}(p_*) - \boldsymbol{p}(p_*)  \bigr\|_2  \\
&\le \frac{1}{2\pi}\kappa_{\rho}\oint_{\Gamma}\frac{|\Pi_r(p_*)|}{|z-p_*||\Pi_r(z)|}|dz|
\end{align*}
We then have to bound
\begin{equation}\@bsphack
